  <main id="main-content">
    <div class="legal-page resources-page">
      <span class="section-label">Resources</span>
      <h1>Keep reading</h1>

      <p>GLASS is a small group, and a lot of good skeptical work happens elsewhere. These publications and podcasts have been
        looking into strange claims, medical myths and the habits of clear thinking for years, some of them for decades. Our blog
        posts often end with a piece or two from them.</p>

      <p>A note on how we use them: we link to articles, not to whole organizations. Our posts are sourced to the research itself,
        such as scientific reviews and peer-reviewed studies, and not to these outlets. Their writers are sometimes sharper with
        believers than we try to be, and sometimes they take sides in debates well outside science. Read them the way you'd read
        anything else: with your own questions ready.</p>

      <h2>Skeptical publications and podcasts</h2>

      <ul class="resources-list">
        <li class="resource-card">
          <span class="quiz-tape" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <h3><a href="https://skepticalinquirer.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Skeptical Inquirer</a></h3>
          <p class="resource-meta">Magazine &middot; Committee for Skeptical Inquiry, a program of the Center for Inquiry &middot; since 1976</p>
          <p>The longest-running outlet here. It's the journal of a committee whose founding members included Carl Sagan, Isaac Asimov,
            Ray Hyman and James Randi. It's strongest on careful investigations of specific cases: a photo, a sighting, a psychic's
            reading. Some articles are for subscribers only.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="resource-card">
          <span class="quiz-tape" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <h3><a href="https://www.skeptic.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Skeptic</a></h3>
          <p class="resource-meta">Magazine and podcast &middot; The Skeptics Society &middot; since 1992</p>
          <p>Long essays, book reviews, interviews and debates. It's good for the bigger picture of why people believe what they do,
            and leans more toward essays and argument than toward investigating individual cases.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="resource-card">
          <span class="quiz-tape" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <h3><a href="https://www.mcgill.ca/oss/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">McGill Office for Science and Society</a></h3>
          <p class="resource-meta">Articles, videos and radio &middot; McGill University &middot; since 1999</p>
          <p>A university office whose motto is "Separating Sense from Nonsense." Its short, friendly articles on food, chemistry and
            health claims explain the science without talking down to anyone, and it takes no funding from any vested interest.
            A good first stop.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="resource-card">
          <span class="quiz-tape" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <h3><a href="https://skeptoid.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Skeptoid</a></h3>
          <p class="resource-meta">Podcast &middot; Brian Dunning, Skeptoid Media &middot; since 2006</p>
          <p>A short weekly podcast that takes on one claim per episode. Episodes come with a written version and a list of
            references, so you can check the sources yourself.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="resource-card">
          <span class="quiz-tape" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <h3><a href="https://www.theskepticsguide.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Skeptics' Guide to the Universe</a></h3>
          <p class="resource-meta">Podcast &middot; Steven Novella and co-hosts, New England Skeptical Society &middot; since 2005</p>
          <p>A weekly science-news show with a panel of hosts, plus quizzes and segments on logical fallacies. Each episode links to
            a free transcript. The same society runs Science-Based Medicine, below.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="resource-card">
          <span class="quiz-tape" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <h3><a href="https://sciencebasedmedicine.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Science-Based Medicine</a></h3>
          <p class="resource-meta">Blog &middot; New England Skeptical Society &middot; since 2008</p>
          <p>Edited by physicians, it looks at medical treatments and products in the light of the clinical evidence. It's one of the
            most thorough skeptical sources on health claims. It isn't medical advice, and neither is anything on our site.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="resource-card">
          <span class="quiz-tape" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <h3><a href="https://skepdic.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Skeptic&rsquo;s Dictionary</a></h3>
          <p class="resource-meta">Reference &middot; Robert Todd Carroll &middot; 1994&ndash;2016</p>
          <p>An A&ndash;Z reference on hundreds of strange beliefs, plus a guide to critical thinking. It's handy for a quick overview
            of a topic. Carroll died in 2016 and the site hasn't been updated since, so check its entries against newer research.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where the evidence comes from</h2>

      <p>Skeptical writers are a good guide, but the strongest evidence comes from reviews that gather all the studies on a question
        and weigh them together. That's the kind of source our posts lean on most.</p>

      <ul class="resources-list">
        <li class="resource-card">
          <span class="quiz-tape" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <h3><a href="https://www.cochrane.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cochrane</a></h3>
          <p class="resource-meta">Systematic reviews &middot; Independent nonprofit network of health researchers</p>
          <p>Cochrane publishes systematic reviews of health treatments, on everything from turmeric for pain to cancer surgery.
            Every review comes with a free plain-language summary, so you can see what the research as a whole says without
            reading the full paper.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>GLASS isn't affiliated with any of these. If there's one you think belongs on this list, <a href="/#contact">tell us</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </main>
